<description>
Recolor every maximal non-overlapping black square pocket blue while leaving grey pixels unchanged.

Input structure
- The grid is a black background with grey obstacle pixels.
- The relevant blank regions are axis-aligned all-black squares of side at least 2.
- The output has the same shape as the input.

Rule
- Search for all all-black square candidate regions of side at least 2.
- Process candidates from largest side length to smallest.
- Paint a candidate blue if it does not overlap any larger square already selected.
- Same-size candidates that survive larger-square blocking must be disjoint.

Key insight
- Long corridors and shifted protrusions are not selected; overlapping larger square rooms claim the cells first.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "all-black square regions of side at least 2 among fixed grey obstacle pixels",
  "transform": "paint blue each square region that does not overlap a larger selected square; preserve grey obstacles and unclaimed black pixels",
  "multi_step": "process candidate side lengths from largest to smallest; same-size surviving candidates are disjoint"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 10x10 [observed]
  colors: black (background) + grey (obstacles) only
  grey_density: 35-41% of cells
  grey_distribution: scattered, 12-17 connected components (4-conn); every row and column has >=1 grey cell [observed]
  square_pockets:
    description: all-black axis-aligned square regions of side >=2, selected greedily largest-first
    count: 3-5 non-overlapping squares total
    side_lengths: 2 or 3; at least one side-2 square present [observed]
    disjointness: after removing cells claimed by larger squares, remaining same-size candidate squares must be mutually non-overlapping (solver asserts this)
  output:
    same grid with each selected square pocket recolored blue; grey and unclaimed black unchanged
    no 2x2 all-black region remains in output [observed]

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first — place blue squares first, then scatter grey obstacles
  steps:
    1. Choose 3-5 square pockets with sides in {2,3}; place them on the 10x10 grid ensuring no two overlap
    2. For each pocket, ensure no larger all-black square could contain it — this is enforced by placing grey obstacles to break any larger open region
    3. Ensure same-size pockets that survive the greedy pass are mutually disjoint (guaranteed by step 1)
    4. Scatter grey pixels on remaining non-pocket cells to reach 35-41% density, ensuring every row/col has >=1 grey cell
    5. Verify no unintended 2x2+ all-black square exists outside the placed pockets — if found, add a grey pixel to break it
    6. Run solver to confirm selected squares match intended pockets; adjust grey if needed
    7. Input = grid with pocket cells as black; output = grid with pocket cells as blue
```
</generation_spec>
